<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Turkmenchay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1826-1828">Russo-Persian War (1826–1828)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Turkmenchay, signed in February 1828, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1826-1828">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1826 to 1828. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> ceded the khanates of Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, confirmed earlier losses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talysh">Talysh</a>, paid a large indemnity and granted Russian subjects legal privileges. It fixed the Aras river as the frontier and encouraged Armenian migration into the ceded lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay.jpg" alt="Treaty of Turkmenchay" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Moshkov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Turkmenchay_by_Moshkov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was the second of a series of agreements between Qajar Iran and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> that forced Iran to cede or recognise Russian influence over territories that had formerly belonged to it. The first was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> of 1813 and the last the Treaty of Akhal of 1881. It was signed on 22 February 1828 in Torkamanchay, a village between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, for Iran by Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a> and Allah-Yar Khan Asef al-Dowleh, chancellor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath-Ali Shah Qajar</a>, and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> by General Ivan Paskievich. As with Gulistan, the treaty was imposed after a Russian military victory, and Paskievich threatened to occupy Tehran in five days unless it was signed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Under Article 4 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> ceded the Erivan Khanate, the Nakhchivan Khanate and the Talysh Khanate, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordubad">Ordubad</a> and Mughan regions, and repeated the cessions of Gulistan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Aras River</a> became the boundary. Iran promised to pay 10 korur in gold or 20 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> rubles, and Persian ships lost the right to navigate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, which passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Russia promised to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a> as heir to the throne, a clause that became moot when he died before Fath-Ali Shah.</p><p>The treaty also recognised capitulation rights for Russian subjects in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to send consular envoys anywhere in the country. Article 15 gave the inhabitants of Iranian Azerbaijan the right to move to Russian districts within one year, and provided for the resettlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The historian Alexander Mikaberidze describes the treaty as the definite acknowledgement of the Persian loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The formerly Iranian territories came under Russian, and later Soviet, control for about 180 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> remains a constituent republic of the Russian Federation, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, which together held most of the territory ceded at Gulistan and Turkmenchay, became independent after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved in 1991.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> from Iranian Azerbaijan used the freedom to emigrate, and in the years 1828 to 1831 some 45,000 are recorded as moving to Russian Armenia. Anti-Russian feeling was widespread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> after the treaty. On 11 February 1829 a mob stormed the Russian embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and killed almost everyone inside, among them the newly appointed ambassador, the playwright Aleksander Griboyedov, who had helped to negotiate the terms. As an apology the shah presented the tsar with the Shah Diamond.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Turkmenchay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A second war in 1826–1828, despite early successes, ended in defeat and the <b>Treaty of Turkmenchay</b>, which surrendered Erivan and Nakhichevan and fixed the frontier on the Aras river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Gulistan of 1813 and the <b>Treaty of Turkmenchay</b> of 1828 it lost Georgia, Armenia and its Caspian navy to Russia and paid an indemnity of three million pounds to the tsar; in the Treaty of Paris of 1857 it agreed to withdraw from Herat.</blockquote><a href="/movement/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Russian troops under Ivan Paskevich captured Erivan in 1827, and the <b>Treaty of Turkmenchay</b> in 1828 ceded the khanate to the Russian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/erivan-khanate">Erivan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Russo-Persian War ended with the <b>Treaty of Turkmenchay</b> in 1828, the Aras became the border, and Qaradagh remained within Iran as a governorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-qaradagh">Khanate of Qaradagh</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Russo-Persian War of 1826-1828, Ehsan Khan Kangarlu went over to Russia, and the <b>Treaty of Turkmenchay</b> in 1828 ceded the khanate to the Russian Empire, which joined it with Erivan in the Armenian Oblast.</blockquote><a href="/state/nakhichevan-khanate">Nakhichevan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After Persian reoccupation in 1826, the <b>Treaty of Turkmenchay</b> in 1828 split Talysh along the Astara river, and the Russian Empire annexed the northern part.</blockquote><a href="/state/talysh-khanate">Talysh Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
